accoreconsole: alfe's deported (load) errors on a source file with a high byte

== Symptom ==

Running the encoding probe under `alfe --autocad --mode batch` (accoreconsole
2022, LISPSYS 0, SYSCODEPAGE ANSI_1252, Windows), an in-engine
`(load "file.lsp")` where file.lsp contains a non-ASCII byte fails:

    source.default.load  = LOAD-ERR   ; file bytes: (setq ENCSRC "A<e9>Z")
    source.utf8.load     = LOAD-ERR   ; ccs= ignored -> same ANSI bytes
    source.utf16le.load  = LOAD-ERR   ; ccs= ignored -> same ANSI bytes

The file is `(setq ENCSRC "AéZ")` with é = 0xE9 (ANSI). The SAME bytes read
back with `(open "r")`+read-char decode fine (read.default.cp = 65 233 172 66),
so accoreconsole itself can decode the byte on READ — it is the (load) path
that errors.

Contrast: on BricsCAD (macOS V26 and Windows V25) the identical probe's
`(load)` decodes UTF-8 (no BOM), UTF-16LE and ANSI source correctly
(source.*.cp = 65 233 90). So the failure is specific to the accoreconsole
backend, not to `(load)` in general.

== Context / likely cause ==

Inside a CAD, `(load …)` does not use the engine's own native loader; it
goes through alfe's DEPORTED `autolisp-source-load` shadow
(autolisp-front-end/source/runtime/autolisp-bootstrap.lsp ~:563; dispatch
autolisp-eval-load-form; registered *CLLOAD_DEFAULT_LOADER*), which reads
the file and ships it over the protocol. accoreconsole's stdin/stdout pipe
is UTF-16LE (fixed by the product), so the deported load's transport /
file-read of a raw high byte is the suspect. This is exactly the
`source`-situation load path that
issues/open/encoding-situations-cli-options.issue §2.1 says must be brought
to the reference algorithm (autolisp-load-file-in-context, which HONOURS
the encoding).

== Repro ==

    make -C autolisp-front-end build-alfe-sbcl
    alfe --autocad --mode batch -l \
      autolisp-front-end/tests/scenarios/entities/encoding-probe.lsp
    # grep the output for source.*.load ; observe LOAD-ERR
    # (needs the Windows/AutoCAD runner: encoding:experiment:autocad:windows)

Measured on CI pipeline 2713419298, job 15586058491 (probe commit 1380ca2).

== To investigate ==

1. Is it the file READ inside autolisp-source-load (wrong/again single-byte
   assumption), or the protocol transport to accoreconsole's UTF-16LE pipe?
2. Does it reproduce under LISPSYS 1/2? (restart-level — separate probe
   invocations; see the issue §8 E1.) If LISPSYS 1/2 loads cleanly, the bug
   is an LISPSYS-0/MBCS interaction. NB (pjb): accoreconsole's `/isolate`
   option prevents sysvar changes from persisting, and alfe passes it — so
   LISPSYS is stuck at 0 under alfe. To even test 1/2, launch accoreconsole
   WITHOUT `/isolate` (or pre-set LISPSYS in the profile/registry). That
   also means the eventual fix cannot rely on `(setvar "LISPSYS" …)` from
   inside an /isolate'd session — it must be a launch/profile precondition.
3. Fix by bringing autolisp-source-load to the reference load algorithm
   (honour the resolved `source` encoding), per the encoding-situations plan.

== Update 2026-07-29 (G3, alfe 1.6.19) ==

The G3 shim (autolisp-source-open-encoded, bootstrap.lsp) now opens the
source file honouring an EXPLICIT -Esource encoding, per backend — for
AutoCAD via the 3rd-arg literal `(open path "r" "utf8")`. This MAY fix this
bug when the user passes `-Esource utf-8` AND LISPSYS is 1/2 (the 3rd arg is
documented only for Unicode LISPSYS; at LISPSYS 0 it falls back to the bare
open via vl-catch-all, so the bug persists there but is no worse). Needs a
Windows/accoreconsole run to confirm: repro with `-Esource utf-8` at LISPSYS
0 vs 1/2 (remember /isolate pins LISPSYS — drop it or pre-set the profile).

== Update 2026-09-26 (real error captured + ROOT CAUSE CORRECTED) ==

The encoding:experiment:autocad:windows job finally ran (AutoCAD 2022
accoreconsole, MR !292 native child 2885828771; the enhanced probe now
surfaces the real message). Two things are now settled.

(A) The real error, across chcp 1252 / 437 / 65001, UTF-8 and C locale, and
all three source-write encodings, is IDENTICAL and is accoreconsole's own
NATIVE (load) failure:

    source.default.load  = LOAD-ERR: La commande CHARGER n'a pas abouti: "encp-src-default.lsp"
    source.utf8.load     = LOAD-ERR: (idem)
    source.utf16le.load  = LOAD-ERR: (idem)
    on-disk bytes confirmed:  ...22 41 e9 5a 22 29   = (setq ENCSRC "A<e9>Z")

Sub-finding: the "w,ccs=UTF-8" / "w,ccs=UTF-16LE" WRITE modes are ignored on
accoreconsole too — all three write variants produced identical single-e9-byte
files, so the write side never re-encodes either.

(B) ROOT-CAUSE CORRECTION. The "Context / likely cause" above (deported
`autolisp-source-load` shadow, protocol transport) is WRONG for what this
probe measures. Verified against the implementation:

  * alfe NEVER redefines the LOAD symbol's function cell. Interception is a
    purely STRUCTURAL, per-form check: autolisp-eval-request-form
    (autolisp-bootstrap.lsp:891) routes a form to the shim only when the form
    it is HANDED is itself a `(load …)` list (autolisp-load-form-p :755).
    *CLLOAD_DEFAULT_LOADER* (:1021) is only a marker setq, never read to
    reroute LOAD.
  * The `-l` scenario ENTRY load IS a top-level `(load "<staged>")`, so THAT
    goes through the shim. But srcprobe's own load — encoding-probe.lsp:104,
    `(vl-catch-all-apply 'load (list path))` — is (a) nested inside the
    srcprobe defun body (never handed to the per-form dispatcher) and (b) not
    even a `(load …)` head. So it resolves accoreconsole's NATIVE LOAD builtin.
    That is why the message is AutoCAD's native "La commande CHARGER".

Consequence: a fix confined to autolisp-source-open-encoded /
autolisp-source-load-run-body-impl would NOT be exercised by this probe. The
shim fix (§3, bring it to the reference algorithm) is still worth doing — it
governs TOP-LEVEL and deported loads, the common real path — but it cannot be
verified by the probe as written, and it does NOT address the nested/native
case this probe exercises.

DECISION NEEDED (pjb) — the fork changes this issue's approach:
  (a) Redefine the LOAD symbol's function cell to autolisp-source-load in the
      engine, so EVERY (load …), nested or not, deports to the byte-safe shim.
      Fixes the general case but has broad blast radius (all loads reroute).
  (b) Restructure the probe so the load is a TOP-LEVEL form (caught + deported)
      — loses the write-then-load-in-one-function measurement design.
  (c) Accept that native accoreconsole LOAD cannot read a high-byte file at
      LISPSYS 0 (an AutoCAD limitation), scope this issue to the shim/top-level
      path, and file the native limitation separately.
Also open regardless: at LISPSYS 0 there may be NO AutoLISP (open) mode that
gives a total single-byte read, so the shim fix itself may need a byte-level
read — empirically untested (needs a runner cycle).

== Related ==

- issues/open/encoding-situations-cli-options.issue (§2.1 load impls, §7.1
  measured source native-load)
- issues/open/cad-load-encoding-macos.issue (the same source-load axis on
  BricsCAD/macOS)

== RESOLVED 2026-09-28 (alfe 2.2.134..2.2.141, MR !313 + !320) ==

Fixed via the alfe-* interface + a path-resolution fix, and VERIFIED on the
Windows AutoCAD runner.

Design (maintainer's call): do NOT shadow the AutoLISP builtins to deport
(load)/(princ)/... -- AutoCAD has no variadic user functions and shadowing
re-enters the protocol loop. Instead alfe exposes fixed-arity alfe-*
operators (alfe-load / alfe-load-onfailure / alfe-load*, alfe-princ* /
alfe-print* / alfe-prin1* + 1-arg conveniences), and alfe-load rewrites the
native calls in the code it loads into their alfe-* form (cons-identity
preserving) and evaluates directly. Code alfe controls (autolisp-test,
outils-autolisp scenarios, the REPL) calls alfe-load; ordinary user code
keeps the native operators. Documented in the alfe user manual, section
"The alfe-* operators (for test and REPL code)".

The actual accoreconsole failure turned out NOT to be the high byte: the
byte-safe read already worked. autolisp-source-resolve-load-path used only
findfile, and accoreconsole's findfile searches the Support File Search Path,
NOT the current directory, so alfe-load of a cwd-relative name resolved to
nil -> "LOAD failed". (The message was masked by autolisp-force-error's
portable (/ 1 0) raise -- confirmed on this pass that accoreconsole has no
(error) primitive, so that mechanism stays; the probe now surfaces
autolisp-effective-error-message.) Fix: a fallback in the resolver that
accepts the path as given (then +".lsp") when it opens, only when findfile
misses -- so BricsCAD/clautolisp are unchanged.

Verification (encoding:experiment:autocad:windows): alfe-load.ascii = OK,
and source.{default,utf8,utf16le}.cp = 65 233 90 -- (setq ENCSRC "A<e9>Z")
loaded via alfe-load reads back as A e-acute Z on accoreconsole, where native
(load) still chokes. BricsCAD (macOS+Windows) and clautolisp: same 65 233 90.
alfe unit suite 1334/1334 with the new regression test
protocol-alfe-load-reads-a-non-ascii-file.
